.IX Title "Lucy::Docs::Tutorial::FieldTypeTutorial 3pm"
.TH Lucy::Docs::Tutorial::FieldTypeTutorial 3pm 2026-09-16 "perl v5.40.1" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
Lucy::Docs::Tutorial::FieldTypeTutorial \- Specify per\-field properties and behaviors.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
The Schema we used in the last chapter specifies three fields:
.PP
.Vb 6
\&    my $type = Lucy::Plan::FullTextType\->new(
\&        analyzer => $easyanalyzer,
\&    );
\&    $schema\->spec_field( name => \*(Aqtitle\*(Aq,   type => $type );
\&    $schema\->spec_field( name => \*(Aqcontent\*(Aq, type => $type );
\&    $schema\->spec_field( name => \*(Aqurl\*(Aq,     type => $type );
.Ve
.PP
Since they are all defined as “full text” fields, they are all searchable –
including the \f(CW\*(C`url\*(C'\fR field, a dubious choice.  Some URLs contain meaningful
information, but these don’t, really:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    http://example.com/us_constitution/amend1.txt
.Ve
.PP
We may as well not bother indexing the URL content.  To achieve that we need
to assign the \f(CW\*(C`url\*(C'\fR field to a different FieldType.
.SS StringType
.IX Subsection "StringType"
Instead of FullTextType, we’ll use a
StringType, which doesn’t use an
Analyzer to break up text into individual fields.  Furthermore, we’ll mark
this StringType as unindexed, so that its content won’t be searchable at all.
.PP
.Vb 2
\&    my $url_type = Lucy::Plan::StringType\->new( indexed => 0 );
\&    $schema\->spec_field( name => \*(Aqurl\*(Aq, type => $url_type );
.Ve
.PP
To observe the change in behavior, try searching for \f(CW\*(C`us_constitution\*(C'\fR both
before and after changing the Schema and re-indexing.
.SS "Toggling ‘stored’"
.IX Subsection "Toggling ‘stored’"
For a taste of other FieldType possibilities, try turning off \f(CW\*(C`stored\*(C'\fR for
one or more fields.
.PP
.Vb 4
\&    my $content_type = Lucy::Plan::FullTextType\->new(
\&        analyzer => $easyanalyzer,
\&        stored   => 0,
\&    );
.Ve
.PP
Turning off \f(CW\*(C`stored\*(C'\fR for either \f(CW\*(C`title\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`url\*(C'\fR mangles our results page,
but since we’re not displaying \f(CW\*(C`content\*(C'\fR, turning it off for \f(CW\*(C`content\*(C'\fR has
no effect – except on index size.
.SS "Analyzers up next"
.IX Subsection "Analyzers up next"
Analyzers play a crucial role in the behavior of FullTextType fields.  In our
next tutorial chapter, AnalysisTutorial, we’ll see how
changing up the Analyzer changes search results.
